diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4961321
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,231 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any signed-in member can open **Set up chat bots** in account settings, then create,
+rename, upload avatars for, or delete their bots. Administrators can manage all bots.
+The bot list shows every accessible bot without pagination.
+
+Bots are users with the `bot` role, a creator, and a random 32-character base58
+`bot_token` (displayed as **Bot code**). They have no email address and receive a
+random, undisclosed password to satisfy the existing users schema. Unlike people,
+bots do not join open rooms by default. Room administrators can select bots in the
+usual member pickers for open and closed rooms. They do not appear in human account
+administration, which cannot promote them to administrators.
+
+The **Add to all open rooms** switch on the new/edit bot form is off by default.
+Turning it on grants the bot membership in every existing open room, and it joins
+new open rooms automatically (including closed rooms converted to open). Turning
+it off keeps all current memberships but stops automatic additions to future
+rooms. The switch never adds the bot to closed rooms or direct conversations.
+Existing bots keep their memberships when upgrading, with the switch initially off.
+
+Deleting a bot deactivates it, rather than destroying it, so historical messages
+keep their author. Only active bots authenticate at the API endpoint. Editing a
+bot does not change its code.
+
+## Bots per member
+
+Administrators can set **Bots per member** in account settings. It defaults to
+**5**, including for existing installations, and accepts non-negative integers.
+Setting it to **0** prevents members from creating any more bots; administrators
+are always exempt.
+
+The quota counts a member's bots that have not been deactivated (deleted). Other
+members' bots and human users do not count. Banned bots still count, since they
+have not been deleted. Lowering the limit never removes bots or their memberships,
+and members can still edit or delete their existing bots. New bots are blocked
+until the member's count falls below the limit, or an administrator raises it.
+
+The bot list hides **Add a chat bot** at the limit and displays **reached the
+limit**. Opening the new-bot URL redirects back to the list. Creation also checks
+the limit in a model validation, so direct POST requests, forms opened before a
+limit change, and ordinary model creation cannot bypass it. The setting uses the
+existing schematized account-settings JSON, so no database migration is needed.
+
+Concurrency note: bot saves run their quota validation and insert in the same
+transaction. The app configures SQLite with `default_transaction_mode: immediate`,
+which serializes competing creations before counting. If moving to another
+database or changing transaction modes, keep this check-and-create operation
+serialized to prevent simultaneous requests from taking the same final slot.
+
+## Posting messages
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/$ROOM_ID/$BOT_CODE/messages"
+```
+
+No session cookie, CSRF token, or JSON envelope is needed. The raw request body is
+UTF-8 plain text; HTML and JSON-looking content are treated literally. For files or
+multiline output use `--data-binary @filename`. The bot must belong to the room.
+Attachments are not currently accepted by this endpoint. `BotMessageRequests`
+runs before Rack method overriding to bypass form/JSON parsing on this endpoint
+only; text containing percent signs, brackets, or `_method` remains ordinary text.
+
+Responses:
+
+- `201` with `Location: /messages/:id`: saved and broadcast using the regular
+  message pipeline, including unread state and push notifications.
+- `422`: blank or invalid UTF-8 text; no message or side effects.
+- `404`: unknown room or a room the bot does not belong to.
+- `401`: unknown, deactivated, banned, or non-bot credential.
+
+## Credential handling
+
+Bot codes are bearer credentials: anyone with one can post as its bot. Use HTTPS,
+store them in CI secrets, and never share them in messages. Deleting the bot revokes
+its code; to replace a compromised credential, delete and recreate the bot.
+
+Codes are only displayed on the authorized bots page. That page uses `no-store`
+and disables Turbo snapshots. Rails parameter filtering covers `bot_token`, and
+`lib/rails_ext/bot_token_filtering.rb` also redacts the credential from Rails'
+`filtered_path`, because ordinary parameter filtering only covers query strings,
+not path segments. Sentry error and transaction request URLs/headers are also redacted.
+
+**Deployment note:** reverse proxies, ingress/access logs, monitoring tools, shell
+history and CI command tracing can still record the original request URL. Configure
+those systems to redact `/rooms/<id>/<code>/messages`, and avoid verbose curl or
+shell tracing for calls containing credentials.
+
+## Receiving room joins
+
+A bot with a **Webhook URL** receives a `joined` event when it is added to an
+open or closed room, whether automatically or by a person. Direct conversations
+never trigger this event. Repeated grants of an existing membership, editing a
+room or bot, and toggling automatic membership off and on do not repeat joins
+for rooms the bot already belongs to.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+There is no `author` or `message` field. Return `200` with `text/plain` or
+`text/html` to post a greeting as the bot in that room, exactly as for mentions
+below, or `204` to say nothing. The same reply URL also supports later messages.
+Joined callbacks run after the membership commits, in background jobs, using the
+same delivery limits and safety checks as mentions. Removing the membership,
+webhook, room, or bot cancels a pending callback; inactive bots are not notified.
+Adding a webhook later does not retroactively notify the bot about existing rooms.
+
+### Membership implementation notes
+
+`Room#memberships.grant_to` keeps bulk inserts for people, but creates bot
+memberships through `find_or_create_by!` so membership callbacks run. The unique
+room/user index prevents duplicate memberships and overlapping automatic/manual
+grants. A membership's `after_create_commit` queues one joined job; updates do
+not queue another. Jobs reference that membership, so a revoked membership's
+pending event cannot be delivered for a replacement membership. Avoid bulk
+inserting bot memberships in future code, since that bypasses these callbacks.
+Webhook delivery is best-effort with no automatic retries, as for mentions.
+
+## Receiving mentions
+
+
+Set the optional **Webhook URL** when creating or editing a bot. Clearing that
+field removes the webhook without changing the bot's code. Use an absolute HTTP
+or HTTPS URL. Bots appear alongside people in the room's @-mention autocomplete;
+the bot must be an active member of that room. Typing its name as ordinary text is
+not a rich-text mention and does not trigger a callback.
+
+After a human's new message commits, Campfire queues one callback per mentioned
+bot, even if the same bot is mentioned several times. HTTP requests run in
+background jobs (Resque in production), never in the message-posting request.
+Edits do not repeat callbacks. Bot-authored messages do not trigger callbacks,
+preventing reply loops.
+
+Campfire sends an HTTP POST with `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/BOT_CODE/messages"
+  },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div class=\"trix-content\">… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`message.body.text` removes only that bot's mention attachments, preserving other
+mentions and formatting as plain text. `message.body.html` is the full rendered
+rich-text body, including the mentions. All paths are relative to your Campfire
+origin. `message.link` opens the room at the original message. `room.reply_to`
+contains this bot's own credential; treat the entire payload as sensitive.
+
+To reply immediately, return `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html`
+(charset parameters are accepted). Campfire posts the body as that bot in the
+same room, including normal broadcasts, unread state, and push notifications.
+Plain text is escaped; HTML uses the usual rich-text rendering and sanitization.
+Return `204` for no message. To reply later, return `204`, then POST raw UTF-8
+plain text to the supplied `room.reply_to` path, as described above.
+
+Other statuses, unsupported/missing content types, blank bodies, invalid UTF-8,
+and replies larger than 5 MiB produce no message. Connection, read, and write
+timeouts are 5, 10, and 5 seconds respectively. Deliveries are best-effort and
+are not automatically retried, avoiding duplicate bot replies. Missing messages,
+removed webhooks, inactive bots, or revoked room membership cancel pending
+callbacks; membership and bot status are also checked before posting a reply.
+
+For server-side request safety, webhook hosts must resolve to public addresses,
+using Campfire's existing `RestrictedHTTP` policy. Requests pin the checked IP
+address and do not follow redirects, so credentials cannot be forwarded to a
+different destination. Private, loopback, and metadata endpoints are not supported;
+use a public HTTPS endpoint or tunnel for local bot development. Failure logs
+identify the bot and error class without logging URLs or payloads.
+
+## Automatic guardrails and suspension
+
+Bot limits are fixed in code, not account settings:
+
+- Across all rooms, a bot can post ten messages in a rolling ten-second window.
+  Both API messages and immediate webhook replies (including joined greetings)
+  count. The next message is rejected and starts a thirty-second cooldown.
+- During that cooldown the posting API returns `429 Too Many Requests` with
+  `Retry-After` rounded up to the remaining whole seconds. Rejected attempts do
+  not extend the cooldown. Five additional posting attempts during the same
+  cooldown suspend the bot; the fifth and subsequent API attempts return `403`.
+  Webhook replies also count as attempts and are discarded while cooling down.
+- Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries suspend the bot. Any non-2xx status
+  (including redirects), timeout, or connection error is a failure. Any 2xx
+  delivery resets the streak, even if its body cannot be posted as a message.
+  Cancelled deliveries are not failures. Both joined and mention events count.
+
+Suspension is distinct from deletion or banning: it preserves messages, room
+memberships, and the bot code. Suspended bots still use their owner's bot slots,
+remain on the bots page with **Suspended** and their reason, return `403` from the
+posting API, and receive no new webhooks. Suspension never expires automatically.
+Only an administrator can use **Reactivate** on the bots page, after a native
+browser confirmation. Reactivation resets the rolling window, cooldown, attempt
+count, and delivery-failure streak, without changing the bot's code or rooms.
+Owners can edit suspended bots, but that does not reactivate them.
+
+### Guardrail implementation notes
+
+`User::Bot::Guardrails` persists a bounded list of the last ten accepted posting
+timestamps and the cooldown/failure state on the user record. All bot posting
+paths must use `post_bot_message!` so checking the quota, inserting the message,
+and recording its timestamp happen in one `with_lock` transaction. Rejections
+raise only after this transaction commits, so counter increments and suspension
+are not rolled back. Keeping timestamps separately from message records also
+prevents message deletion from evading a window's limit.
+
+Webhook HTTP requests run outside database locks. Availability is checked again
+when a queued job begins and before posting its reply; an already in-flight
+request cannot be recalled, but suspension prevents its reply. Delivery results
+are recorded under the same bot lock. Rejected replies are not failed deliveries.
+The existing best-effort/no-retry policy remains unchanged.
diff --git a/lib/bot_message_requests.rb b/lib/bot_message_requests.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7398665
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/bot_message_requests.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# curl -d sends a form content type, but bot messages are always raw text.
+# Keep both Rack's method overriding and Rails' instrumentation from parsing it.
+class BotMessageRequests
+  def self.filtered_path(path)
+    path&.sub(%r{(/rooms/[^/?]+/)[^/?]+(/messages(?:\.[^/?]+)?(?:[/?]|\z))}, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+  end
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if env["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST" && env["PATH_INFO"].match?(%r{\A/rooms/[^/]+/[^/]+/messages(?:\.[^/]+)?/?\z})
+      env["rack.request.form_hash"] = {}
+      env["action_dispatch.request.request_parameters"] = {}
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+end
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/bot_token_filtering.rb b/lib/rails_ext/bot_token_filtering.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f1bf1f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/bot_token_filtering.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
+# Parameter filtering doesn't cover credentials embedded in a URL's path.
+ActiveSupport.on_load(:action_dispatch_request) do
+  prepend Module.new {
+    def filtered_path
+      BotMessageRequests.filtered_path(super)
+    end
+  }
+end
